course: biology
lesson: 347
title: Sugar, chocolate, tea and coffee: the plants behind treats
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Every sweet treat starts as a plant. Sugar comes from the stems of sugar cane, a giant grass taller than a grown-up, or from the pale roots of sugar beet in cool countries. The plant stored that sugar as food for itself, and we squeeze it out and boil it into crystals.
explain: Chocolate starts as a bitter bean. Cocoa pods grow straight out of the trunk of the cacao tree, and inside each one sit about 20 to 50 beans in sweet white pulp. Only after fermenting, drying and roasting do they start to smell of chocolate.
explain: Tea and coffee are plants too. Green tea and black tea come from the very same bush, and every coffee bean is a seed, usually one of two lying flat side to flat side in a red coffee cherry.
explain: Many of these plants make caffeine or similar chemicals to defend themselves from insects. That is why coffee and energy drinks keep people awake, and why chocolate can make a dog very ill.
fact: Sugar cane is a giant grass that stores sugar in its stems; the stems are crushed for juice, which is boiled until sugar crystals form, and Brazil and India grow the most.
fact: The crushed cane stems left over are burned to power the sugar mills.
fact: Most sugar made in Britain comes from sugar beet, a pale root that grows in cool countries where cane cannot, and white sugar from beet and cane is the same.
fact: Cocoa pods grow on the trunk and big branches, hold about 20 to 50 beans in sweet pulp, and their tiny flowers are pollinated by small flies called midges.
fact: Fresh cocoa beans taste bitter and smell of chocolate only after fermenting, drying and roasting.
fact: In Mexico and Central America cocoa was drunk bitter, frothy and sometimes spicy, and the Aztecs used cocoa beans as money; today most cocoa grows in West Africa, especially Ivory Coast and Ghana.
fact: Chocolate contains theobromine, which dogs break down very slowly, so it can make them very ill, dark chocolate most of all.
fact: All real tea comes from one plant, Camellia sinensis; green tea is dried quickly, black tea is crushed and left to darken, and pickers usually take the top two leaves and a bud.
fact: Fruit and herbal teas are not really tea, just other plants in hot water.
fact: Tea came from China, and in the 1800s the British started big tea gardens in India, in Assam and Darjeeling; Britons are reported to drink about 100 million cups a day.
fact: A coffee cherry usually holds two beans facing flat side to flat side, sometimes one round bean called a peaberry, and the beans are green until roasted.
fact: Coffee plants first grew wild in Ethiopia; the story of a goatherd named Kaldi whose goats danced after eating the berries is a legend nobody can prove.
fact: Caffeine is a bitter defence that helps protect plants from insects; it keeps people awake and hits children harder because their bodies are smaller, and many British shops will not sell energy drinks to under-16s.
exercise: Which part of sugar cane holds the sugar? | The stems | The roots underground | The flowers at the top | The small seeds
exercise: How is sugar made from cane juice? | It is boiled until sugar crystals form | It is frozen hard and then chopped into white cubes | It is dried in the sun on leaves | It is mixed with salt and pressed
exercise: Which plant gives most of the sugar made in Britain? | Sugar beet | Sugar cane | Maple trees | Sugar palms
exercise: Is sugar from beet different from sugar from cane? | No, white sugar from both is the same | Yes, beet sugar always comes out a deep red colour | Yes, cane sugar is not sweet | Yes, beet sugar tastes of soil
exercise: Where do cocoa pods grow? | On the trunk and big branches of the tree | Under the ground, the same way potatoes do | On the tips of the highest twigs | On a bush in the desert
exercise: About how many beans are inside one cocoa pod? | About 20 to 50 | About 2 | About 500 | Exactly one
exercise: What do fresh cocoa beans taste like? | Bitter | Like milk chocolate | Like strawberries | Salty, like crisps
exercise: What did the Aztecs use cocoa beans for besides drinks? | As money | As building bricks | As jewellery for horses | As fishing bait
exercise: Where is most cocoa grown today? | West Africa, especially Ivory Coast and Ghana | Britain and Ireland, in big heated glasshouses | Mexico only | Antarctica
exercise: Why is chocolate dangerous for dogs? | Dogs break down its theobromine very slowly | It is too sticky and gets stuck on their teeth | Dogs are allergic to sugar | It makes all their fur fall out
exercise: What is the difference between green tea and black tea? | Same bush, but black tea is crushed and left to darken | They come from two different trees that grow in different countries | Green tea is unripe black tea berries | Black tea is burnt green tea
exercise: What do tea pickers usually take from each shoot? | The top two leaves and a bud | The whole branch with all its leaves | Only the roots under the soil | The oldest leaves at the bottom
exercise: Where did the British start big tea gardens in the 1800s? | In India, in Assam and Darjeeling | In the hills and glens of Scotland, near the lochs | In Canada | In Egypt
exercise: How many beans are usually inside one coffee cherry? | Two | Only one | About ten | Twenty
exercise: What colour are coffee beans before they are roasted? | Green | Shiny black | Snow white | Bright red
exercise: Where did coffee plants first grow wild? | Ethiopia | Brazil's rainforests | Southern Italy | Colombia's mountains
exercise: Why does a coffee plant make caffeine? | It is a bitter defence against insects | To help the plant stay awake all through the night | To make its flowers smell nice | To turn the beans brown
exercise: Why does the caffeine in an energy drink hit a child harder than a grown-up? | A child's body is smaller, so the same amount has a bigger effect | Children have no caffeine in their blood | Caffeine only works on children and stops working once you grow up | Energy drinks are stronger for children
test: In Charlie and the Chocolate Factory, chocolate flows in a river ready to drink. What does real chocolate start as? | Bitter beans in pods that must be fermented, dried and roasted | A sweet brown sap from a chocolate tree | Brown milk from special cows | Sugar mixed with brown soil
test: Count them: a tea picker picks from 4 shoots, taking two leaves and a bud from each. How many bits does she pick? | 12 | 8 | 4 | 16
test: Count them: 5 coffee cherries, all with two beans and none a peaberry. How many beans? | 10 | 5 | 7 | 20
test: «chuck the dog a bit of my chocolate, he's begging» What is the best answer? | No, chocolate can make dogs very ill; keep it out of reach | Yeah, a little bit is fine for dogs, as long as it is milk chocolate | Yes, but only dark chocolate | No, but give him coffee instead
test: «peppermint tea is just tea with mint in» Is that right? | No, herbal teas are other plants in hot water, not real tea | Yes, it is green tea with a few mint leaves added to it | Yes, all teas come from the mint plant | No, peppermint tea is coffee
test: «the goats found coffee and went mental dancing, that's in the history books» What is the best answer? | It is an old legend; coffee did grow wild in Ethiopia, but nobody can prove the goat story | Yes, it is a proven fact with dates | No, coffee was found in Brazil by sailors | No, goats never eat plants
test: «why's Brazil burning all that cane stuff» What are the leftover crushed stems used for? | They are burned to power the sugar mills | They are burned to scare the insects away from the fields | They are thrown into the sea | They are made into chocolate
test: Why did cool countries look for another sugar plant than cane? | Cane needs heat, but beet grows well in cool places | Cane sugar was found to be poisonous for people in cold places | Beet sugar is much sweeter | Cane cannot be crushed
test: A child drinks a can of cola at bedtime. Why might she feel tired the next day? | The caffeine kept her awake and spoiled her sleep | Cola has no sugar, so she had no energy left for the morning | Cola makes people grow overnight | Bubbles stop the heart for a while
test: How many cocoa pods of about 25 beans would you need for 100 beans? | 4 | 25 | 10 | 2
test: In a cartoon, a goofy coffee bean is brown and shiny on the bush. What is wrong? | Coffee beans are green until roasted, and they sit inside red cherries | Nothing, coffee grows brown | Coffee beans grow in pods on the trunk | Coffee beans grow under the ground
test: Why might a hot drink like tea be so popular in a cold, rainy country? | It warms people up and became a habit once tea gardens gave a steady supply | Tea only grows in rainy countries | Rain water makes the best tea | People there cannot drink cold drinks
exam: Identify the part of the sugar cane plant in which sugar is stored. | The stems | The roots | The flowers | The leaves exclusively
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relationship between green and black tea? | Both are derived from the same plant, black tea having been crushed and permitted to darken | They are derived from two unrelated species | Black tea is produced by burning green tea | Green tea is obtained from the unripe berries of the tea plant
exam: Ascertain the reason for which chocolate is hazardous to dogs. | Dogs metabolise theobromine very slowly, so that it accumulates and affects the heart | Chocolate is excessively adhesive and adheres to the teeth of dogs, causing them to decay rapidly | Dogs are universally allergic to sugar | Chocolate causes the immediate loss of canine fur
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the function of caffeine in the coffee plant. | It is a bitter substance that assists in protecting the plant from insects | It enables the plant to remain active during the night | It produces the colour of the roasted bean | It attracts goats to disperse the seeds
